    {
      "title": "Homochirality as an error-correcting molecular code: a controlled channel-capacity assay for chiral electron-transfer assemblies",
      "motivation": "The original question asks whether chirality is a defining requirement for life or a contingent feature of terrestrial biochemistry. Current origin-of-life work explains how chiral bias can be amplified in special reactions [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3210500841), how network-level symmetry breaking may arise in large molecular systems [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3120596068), and how homochiral peptides might form and persist in aqueous prebiological settings [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4313574776). However, these advances do not quantify the functional advantage that homochirality provides to life-relevant processes such as molecular recognition, catalysis, replication fidelity, or electron transfer. The seed work suggests homochirality can affect electron transfer in protein assemblies [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4384627421), but it does not yet provide a general, falsifiable criterion for when homochirality becomes necessary rather than accidental. The proposed idea transfers the concept of molecular codes as noisy information channels from information theory and molecular-code evolution [Author et al., 2008](https://openalex.org/W2115810426), and the statistical-mechanical treatment of code emergence as symmetry breaking [Author et al., 2010](https://openalex.org/W2156278460). In this view, homochirality is not merely a chemical bias but a coding constraint that reduces cross-handed recognition noise and increases the channel capacity of energy and information transfer. This addresses the central bottleneck by converting the question why life requires chirality into a measurable hypothesis about fidelity, noise, and functional throughput in chiral molecular assemblies.",
