{
  "ideas": [
    {
      "title": "Homochirality as a parity-check error-correcting code for electron-transfer protein assemblies",
      "motivation": "The central question is not only how homochirality could emerge but whether it is functionally required. Existing studies show that chiral uniformity can affect electron transfer in protein-like assemblies [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4384627421), and that symmetry breaking can arise in nonequilibrium networks [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3120596068), autocatalytic reactions [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3210500841), and prebiotic ligation chemistries [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4390532605). However, a missing link is a quantitative mechanism showing why homochirality becomes obligatory for life-relevant functions such as reliable molecular recognition and electron transfer. Homochiral peptides may be stable in some prebiotic settings [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4313574776), but stability alone does not explain error suppression, coordination, or functional thresholds. This proposal imports a mechanism from computer science: noisy molecular-code optimization in polymer networks [Author et al., 2008](https://openalex.org/W2137931210). It treats homochirality as a parity-check code: uniform handedness defines allowed codewords, whereas heterochiral mismatches become forbidden codewords that can be detected and rejected. This directly addresses the bottleneck that current work explains chiral amplification but not the functional necessity of chirality.",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve whether homochirality is required for robust electron transfer by testing a physical error-correcting code rather than merely measuring average yield; it can be divided into 4 parts: (1) a minimal chiral electron-transfer assembly with allowed and forbidden codewords, (2) a parity-check reader/checkpoint that selectively stabilizes allowed codewords and rejects forbidden ones, (3) a polymer-network percolation model predicting a sharp functional threshold, and (4) a controlled assay that varies chiral purity while ruling out nonspecific stabilization and mass-transport effects.",
      "proposed_method": [
        {
          "name": "Minimal chiral codeword electron-transfer assembly",
          "description": "Design coiled-coil peptide building blocks in which three stereocenters at a recognition interface encode LLL or DDD as allowed codewords and mixed L/D triplets as forbidden codewords. A donor and acceptor are placed at fixed positions so that electron transfer depends on the geometry of the codeword interface. This makes chirality an explicit coding variable and is motivated by evidence that homochirality can alter electron transfer in protein assemblies [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4384627421). Molecular dynamics and Marcus-type analysis will be used to relate codeword identity to donor-acceptor distance, orientational variance, electronic coupling, and reorganization energy."
        },
        {
          "name": "Parity-check reader and active rejection checkpoint",
          "description": "Borrow noisy molecular-code optimization from computer science [Author et al., 2008](https://openalex.org/W2137931210) to implement a decoder: a trivalent reader molecule or peptide-DNA adaptor binds only the allowed homochiral codeword geometry and crosslinks it into an assembly. Forbidden heterochiral codewords fail the parity check, are not protected, and are removed by a secondary dissociation, proteolysis, or competitive-displacement step. This converts heterochiral mismatches into detectable errors rather than merely less efficient components, addressing the prior gap that information metrics alone do not specify an error-correction mechanism."
        },
        {
          "name": "Percolation-threshold model of functional necessity",
          "description": "Represent the assembly as a polymer network whose nodes are codewords and whose links are reader-stabilized contacts. Electron transfer is functional only when a connected cluster of allowed, reader-stabilized nodes spans the system and maintains electronic coupling above a chosen threshold. Using polymer-network statistics and noisy-code optimization [Author et al., 2008](https://openalex.org/W2137931210), predict a critical allowed-codeword fraction p_c below which error correction fails and electron-transfer fidelity collapses. This model connects the functional threshold to system-level symmetry-breaking constraints in large molecular systems [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3120596068) and to prebiotic ligation/amplification scenarios [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4390532605)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Necessity-versus-contingency assay",
          "description": "Define a life-required performance criterion, for example an electron-transfer rate or error rate needed for a protocellular energy flux. Compare the parity-check homochiral code against racemic non-coded controls, achiral flexible-linker controls, and opposite-handed controls under the same criterion. If only the homochiral code reaches the criterion, chirality is functionally necessary for that definition of life; if alternative codes reach it, chirality is contingent. This framing responds to the origin-of-life motivation that homochiral peptides could form and persist in aqueous crustal environments [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4313574776), while extending the question from formation to functional requirement."
        }
      ],
      "analogy_mapping": [
        {
          "inspiration": "Casting polymer nets to optimize noisy molecular codes",
          "concept": "Noisy molecular recognition framed as an optimizable code embedded in a polymer network, including forbidden or error-prone codewords, decoder constraints, and robustness trade-offs.",
          "adaptation": "Homochiral triplets are treated as allowed parity-check codewords; heterochiral triplets are forbidden codewords. A reader/checkpoint decodes the code, stabilizes allowed codewords, rejects forbidden codewords, and creates a percolation threshold for electron-transfer fidelity.",
          "citation": "[Author et al., 2008](https://openalex.org/W2137931210)"
        }
      ],
      "experiment_plan": [
        "State the primary hypothesis: a homochiral parity-check code produces a sharp threshold in electron-transfer fidelity as the fraction of allowed codewords increases, whereas non-coded or randomized chiral mixtures show only gradual improvement. The primary factor in phase 1 is the allowed-codeword fraction p; all other variables are held fixed.",
        "Synthesize peptide codeword building blocks with identical sequence, donor, acceptor, and length, differing only in the three chiral recognition positions. Prepare allowed LLL, allowed DDD, and each forbidden mixed triplet. Verify composition and absence of racemization by chiral HPLC and mass spectrometry.",
        "Assemble samples at fixed total peptide concentration, pH, ionic strength, temperature, and assembly time across p = 0, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 0.95, and 1.0 in the active parity-check reader condition. Measure electron-transfer rates, yields, and fast-fraction using time-resolved fluorescence or photoinduced electrochemistry.",
        "Run strict controls at the same p values: no reader; scrambled or non-selective reader; physical mixture of preformed allowed and forbidden assemblies without reader-mediated linking; randomized codeword libraries with the same p but no three-position parity rule; allowed-only and forbidden-only samples. These controls separate parity-check error correction from simple concentration, adhesion, or nonspecific stabilization effects.",
        "Characterize the microscopic mechanism: use smFRET, DEER/EPR, circular dichroism, and molecular dynamics to measure donor-acceptor distance distributions, orientational order, secondary structure, and aggregate size. Compute electronic coupling and reorganization energy to test whether forbidden codewords reduce electron transfer by increasing orientational disorder rather than by changing concentration.",
        "Rule out trivial confounders: quantify assembly concentration by SEC-MALS or absorbance to exclude mass-action effects; measure donor and acceptor leaching and photobleaching; test electron-transfer signals in reader-only and donor/acceptor-only controls; monitor racemization over the experiment; use microfluidic flow or dilution controls to show that threshold is not caused by mass transport or surface adsorption.",
        "Test active rejection: pulse forbidden codewords into preformed allowed networks and measure dissociation or proteolytic removal with and without the checkpoint. Controls include inactive reader, protease-inhibited samples, and non-cleavable codeword analogs. This confirms that forbidden codewords are actively rejected rather than passively less conductive.",
        "Compare measured p-dependence to the percolation model. Fit p_c, reader affinity, and coupling threshold. Confirm that the active code shows a sharp transition while no-reader and randomized controls do not, before varying secondary parameters such as donor-acceptor spacing, reader affinity, salt, temperature, racemization rate, or cross-polymer chiral coordination.",
